>=20
> The lojban "emotional" system is very rich and I feel that future
> lojbanic poets and musicians will find great ways to use it.
> However, not being a poet myself, I feel I miss some of the lojban
> potentials not being able to go beyond the most simple emotions.
>=20
> So; i made some research and found psychology studies on the Internet
> that could be help me (and hopefully others) to better understand the
> lojban emotional systems.
>=20
> One I found interesting is Plutchik's wheel of emotions:
> http://www.ebookopen.com/greener/images/Plutchikfig6.gif , It is
> somewhat lojbanic in the sense that complex emotions are considered as
> combination of simpler emotioins (for example "love" is "joy"+"trust")
>=20
> To my shame, I found I could translate very few of those "basic
> emotions", it would be great if someone could help me translate them,
> I'll try to devise some graphical representation for a cheat sheet!
>=20
> Anyone?
>=20

Some years ago, I took a stab at this.=A0 It turned out to be more difficul=
t=20
than I expected.=A0 Lojban emotions don't map very well onto Plutchik's whe=
el.

See attached speadsheet.

